.TH SINK 2
.SH NAME
Sink, sink_from, bytes, str, reset, put_byte, put_str, put_pad, put_uint, put_int, put_hex, put_ptr, put_size \- format into a fixed buffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libodin"
.PP
.nf
.B "Sink :: struct { buf: []u8, len: int, overflowed: bool }"
.B "sink_from :: proc(buf: []u8) -> Sink"
.B "bytes :: proc(s: ^Sink) -> []u8"
.B "str :: proc(s: ^Sink) -> string"
.B "reset :: proc(s: ^Sink)"
.B "put_byte :: proc(s: ^Sink, b: u8)"
.B "put_str :: proc(s: ^Sink, text: string)"
.B "put_pad :: proc(s: ^Sink, b: u8, count: int)"
.B "put_uint :: proc(s: ^Sink, value: u64, base: u64 = 10, width: int = 0, upper := false)"
.B "put_int :: proc(s: ^Sink, value: i64, base: u64 = 10)"
.B "put_hex :: proc(s: ^Sink, value: u64, width: int = 0)"
.B "put_ptr :: proc(s: ^Sink, p: rawptr)"
.B "put_size :: proc(s: ^Sink, bytes_count: u64)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.B Sink
is a fixed byte buffer, given by the caller, plus a write cursor.
Nothing here allocates: this package is what the kernel and an early
userland program format text with before an allocator exists, or from
inside a fault handler, where discovering there is none would itself be
a fault.
.PP
.I Sink_from
wraps
.I buf
with the cursor at its start.
.I Bytes
and
.I str
answer the portion written so far, as a slice or a string that aliases
.IR buf ;
.I reset
puts the cursor back to the start and clears
.IR overflowed .
.PP
Every
.B put_
procedure appends to the sink.
Writing past the end of
.I buf
sets
.I overflowed
and drops what does not fit, rather than growing or panicking: a panic
message that is too long still prints what fits, instead of faulting
inside the panic path.
.PP
.I Put_byte
and
.I put_str
append one byte or a string.
.I Put_pad
appends
.I count
copies of
.IR b ,
and does nothing for a non-positive
.IR count .
.I Put_uint
formats
.I value
in
.I base
(2 to 16), zero-padded to at least
.I width
digits, using upper-case hex letters when
.I upper
is set.
.I Put_int
is the same for a signed value, writing a leading
.B \-
first.
.I Put_hex
writes an
.B 0x
prefixed value, and
.I put_ptr
writes a pointer as sixteen hex digits, so a column of addresses in a
log lines up.
.I Put_size
writes a byte count in the largest binary unit that keeps the integer
part non-zero, with one fractional digit, so a boot log reads
.B 3.9 GiB
rather than a number nobody parses at a glance.
.SH EXAMPLES
The kernel's boot log builds each line into a sink before handing it to
the log:
.IP
.EX
sink := begin(&klog)
libodin.put_str(&sink, "framebuffer ")
libodin.put_uint(&sink, u64(screen.width))
libodin.put_str(&sink, "x")
libodin.put_uint(&sink, u64(screen.height))
emit(&klog, .Ok, &sink)
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libodin/format.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR print (2),
.IR bio (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Overflowed
is set on the sink itself once a write does not fit; nothing here
answers an error value.
